// The words of iron's smith room ("How did people get iron from rock?"), in English, European Portuguese,
// Polish and German, kid (Explorer) and sci (Scientist). The picture's own story stays in
// src/content/boards/Fe.js (the easel shows it); the room lets the visitor do it.
// Sources and checks: bloomery operating range about 1100 to 1300 °C, a fayalite-rich slag that runs near
// 1180 to 1200 °C (the Wealden Iron Research Group's experiments; EXARC EuroREA 6, 2009); experimental
// blooms from under 1 kg to 13.5 kg, 5 to 45% of the ore (the Wareham Forge smelts, 2005 to 2014); pack
// carburising about 1 mm in a few hours at 900 to 950 °C, depth growing as the square root of time;
// iron's Curie point 770 °C and its melting point 1538 °C (src/content/Fe.js, elements.json); A1 727 °C;
// the Iron Age in Britain from about 800 BC. Glow colours against temperature differ between sources by
// tens of degrees: given as "about". The magnet test is a modern smith's practice ("smiths today").
// Placeholders: {kg}, {t} (a temperature with its unit), {n}, {max}, {h}, {c} (carbon, %), {d} (mm).
